{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2612 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c4717d5815f30873c617689e78ef2dbe63cec7acbf5988a94624c6fd0eb67884" :edb/db-before-t 2611 :edb/db-after-t 2612 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535924 50 #inst "2026-10-09T10:48:08.464Z" 13194139535924 true] [17592186164742 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164747 13194139535924 true] [17592186164749 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164753 13194139535924 true] [17592186166497 1100 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + 9\\lambda y = 0, \\qquad y(0) = y(\\pi) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi, \\quad \\lambda \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem." 13194139535924 true] [17592186166497 1100 "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y'' + 9\\lambda y = 0, \\qquad y(0) = y(\\pi) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi, \\quad \\lambda \\geq 0$.\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem." 13194139535924 false] [17592186166497 1145 "We consider the cases $\\lambda = 0$ and $\\lambda > 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions.\n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 0\n$$\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation\n$y'' = 0$.\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions\n\n$$\ny(0) = y(\\pi) = 0\n$$\n\nyields the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0$. Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda = 0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda > 0$.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set\n\n$$\ny = e^{m x}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} + 9λ &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nm = \\pm 3\\textrm i\\sqrt{\\lambda}\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda})\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}) + B\\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda})\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\cos (0) + B\\sin (0) = 0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A = 0$. So, we have\n\n$$\ny = B\\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda})\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\sin (3\\pi\\sqrt{\\lambda}) = 0\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require\n\n$$\n3\\pi\\sqrt{\\lambda} = n\\pi\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in \\mathbb N$. This gives the set of eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_n = \\dfrac{n^2}{9}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are\n\n$$\ny_n(x) = B_n \\sin(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}), \\qquad B_n \\in\\mathbb R\n$$" 13194139535924 false] [17592186166497 1145 "We consider the cases $\\lambda = 0$ and $\\lambda > 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda = 0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0) = y(\\pi) = 0$ yields the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda = 0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda > 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m = \\pm 3\\textrm i\\sqrt{\\lambda} .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda})\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}) + B\\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\cos (0) + B\\sin (0) = 0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A = 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny = B\\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\sin (3\\pi\\sqrt{\\lambda}) = 0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n3\\pi\\sqrt{\\lambda} = n\\pi\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in \\mathbb N.$ This gives the set of eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_n = \\dfrac{n^2}{9}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\ny_n(x) = B_n \\sin(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}), \\qquad B_n \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$" 13194139535924 true] [17592186166498 1100 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0, \\quad y'(1) = 0, \\quad y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0, \\quad 1 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad \\lambda \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants." 13194139535924 true] [17592186166498 1100 "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y = 0, \\quad y'(1) = 0, \\quad y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0, \\quad 1 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad \\lambda \\in\\mathbb R$.\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants." 13194139535924 false] [17592186166498 1145 "We consider the cases $\\lambda = 0$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda \\neq 0\n$$\n\nseparately, since they correspond to different solutions.\n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 0\n$$\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation\n$y'' = 0$.\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y'(1) =0$ and\n\n$$\ny(2) + 2y'(2) = 0\n$$\n\nyields the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0$. Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda = 0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider\n\n$$\n\\lambda \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set\n\n$$\ny = e^{m x}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} - 18λm + 81λ^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(m - 9λ)(m - 9λ) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root\n\n$$\nm = 9\\lambda\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{9\\lambda x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = xe^{9\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{9\\lambda x} + Bxe^{9\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 9Aλe^{9λx} + Be^{9λx} + 9Bλxe^{9λx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y'(1) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9Aλe^{9λ} + Be^{9λ} + 9Bλe^{9λ} &= 0 \\\\\n9Aλ + B + 9Bλ &= 0 \\\\\n(9λ)A + (1 + 9λ)B &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition\n\n$$\ny(2) + 2y'(2) = 0\n$$\n\nyields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAe^{18λ} + 2Be^{18λ} + 2(9Aλe^{18λ} + Be^{18λ} + 18Bλe^{18λ}) &= 0 \\\\\nA + 2B + 2(9Aλ + B + 18Bλ) &= 0 \\\\\nA + 2B + 18Aλ + 2B + 36Bλ &= 0 \\\\\n(1 + 18λ)A + (4 + 36λ)B &= 0 \\\\\n(1 + 18λ)A + 4(1 + 9λ)B &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{(9λ)A + (1 + 9λ)B = 0; (1 + 18λ)A + 4(1 + 9λ)B = 0\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9λ & 1 + 9λ \\\\ 1 + 18λ & 4(1 + 9λ)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9λ \\cdot 4(1 + 9λ) - (1 + 9λ)(1 + 18λ) &= 0 \\\\\n(1 + 9λ)[36λ - (1 + 18λ)] &= 0 \\\\\n(1 + 9λ)[36λ - 1 - 18λ] &= 0 \\\\\n(1 + 9λ)(18λ - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = -\\dfrac19 \\textrm{and} \\lambda_2 = \\dfrac1{18}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider $\\lambda_1 = -\\dfrac19$. Then, we have the solution\n$y_{1}(x)|= Ae^{9λ_{1}x} + Bxe^{9λ_{1}x}; = Ae^{-x} + Bxe^{-x}$\nand substituting $\\lambda_1$ into\n$(9\\lambda)A + (1 + 9\\lambda)B =0$\nwe get\n$-A + \\left(1 -1\\right)B =0$\nwhich gives $A = 0$, and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n$y_1(x) = Bxe^{-x}$.\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 = \\dfrac1{18}$, we have\n$y_{2}(x)|= Ae^{9λ_{2}x} + Bxe^{9λ_{2}x}; = Ae^{x/2} + Bxe^{x/2}$\nand substituting $\\lambda_2$ into\n$(9\\lambda)A + (1 + 9\\lambda)B =0$\nwe get\n$\\dfrac12A + \\dfrac32 B = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad A = -3B$\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n$y_{2}(x)|=-3Be^{x/2} + Bxe^{x/2}; = Be^{x/2}(x - 3); = Ce^{x/2}(x - 3)$\nwhere we have set $C = B$.\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, respectively, are $Bxe^{-x} \\textrm{and} Ce^{x/2}(x-3)$." 13194139535924 false] [17592186166498 1145 "We consider the cases $\\lambda = 0$ and $\\lambda \\neq 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda = 0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y'(1) =0$ and $y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0$ yields the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda = 0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\neq 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-18\\lambda m+81{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-9\\lambda )(m-9\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m = 9\\lambda.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{9\\lambda x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = xe^{9\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{9\\lambda x} + Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =9A\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda x}+B{e}^{9\\lambda x}+9B\\lambda x{e}^{9\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y'(1) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9A\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda }+B{e}^{9\\lambda }+9B\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 9A\\lambda +B+9B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (9\\lambda )A+(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{18\\lambda }+2B{e}^{18\\lambda }+2(9A\\lambda {e}^{18\\lambda }+B{e}^{18\\lambda }+18B\\lambda {e}^{18\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(9A\\lambda +B+18B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+18A\\lambda +2B+36B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+(4+36\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+4(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(9\\lambda )A+(1+9\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+4(1+9\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/assets/29df2dce71db62482d1664cc2f4033dce94574fb1903d80828f4f1d367f8c9a9.png)\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+9\\lambda )-(1+9\\lambda )(1+18\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )[36\\lambda -(1+18\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )[36\\lambda -1-18\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )(18\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 = -\\dfrac19  \\textrm{ and } \\lambda_2 = \\dfrac1{18}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider $\\lambda_1 = -\\dfrac19.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting $\\lambda_1$ into\n\n$$\n(9\\lambda)A  + (1 + 9\\lambda)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A  + \\left(1 -1\\right)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A = 0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = Bxe^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 = \\dfrac1{18},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting $\\lambda_2$ into\n\n$$\n(9\\lambda)A  + (1 + 9\\lambda)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac12A + \\dfrac32 B = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad A = -3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C = B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding 