alue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+4{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 2\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n2\\lambda =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" 13194139535924 true] [17592186167207 1017 #edb/ref 17592186164777 13194139535924 false] [17592186167207 1017 #edb/ref 17592186164782 13194139535924 false] [17592186167207 1017 #edb/ref 17592186374588 13194139535924 true] [17592186167207 1017 #edb/ref 17592186374593 13194139535924 true] [17592186167207 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535924 true] [17592186167207 1100 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8\\lambda {y}^{′}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139535924 true] [17592186167207 1100 "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y^{″} - 8λy^{′} + 16λ^{2}y = 0, y^{′}